>
> One of the first things I would do is consider how much of that $80k
> salary you had left over each month after you paid all of your expenses.
> Once you have this baseline, we can look at how much would be required to
> match it. It's probably a lot lower than you think.
>
>
> Matching a high salary in the countries you've specified is going to be
> rough. But it's entirely possible to do it somewhere like China.
>
> Generally speaking I encourage no one to ever work for less than 200 RMB
> or $30US per hour in China because it's just not necessary. I very rarely
> see training center contracts that pencil out. The best positions are in
> public and private K-12 and universities and colleges. Once you've worked
> your way through the manual, please let me know what you're thinking and =
we
> can go from there.
>
> Many people also find that they're not getting as many hits and as high
> quality of offers as they'd like because their resumes are off. You can
> upgrade your package if desired within the first 30 days and we can work =
on
> getting all of your documents looking professional and polished.
>
> Ideally I'd like to see you get at very least double what this contract i=
s
> offering in terms of hourly compensation. I look forward to continuing th=
is
> conversation with you.
>
> Ben
